[b][size=5]Tullius CTD Logger (SkyrimDiag)[/size][/b]

Diagnostic tool for Skyrim SE/AE.
When the game hits a [b]CTD[/b], [b]freeze[/b], or [b]infinite loading (ILS)[/b] state, it captures dumps and surrounding clues, then generates a report that opens with a [b]quick answer[/b] in plain words (what happened, the suspected mod, one thing to try); the summary, evidence, and checklist follow below it.

[b]Quick Links[/b]
[url=https://github.com/servaltullius/Tullius_ctd_loger]GitHub (source)[/url] · [url=https://github.com/servaltullius/Tullius_ctd_loger/releases/latest]Latest release[/url] · [url=https://github.com/servaltullius/Tullius_ctd_loger/issues]Issue reporting[/url]

[b][size=4]What It Does[/size][/b]
[list]
[*]Captures [b]dumps (.dmp)[/b] for CTD, freeze, and ILS scenarios
[*]Records [b]blackbox events[/b], [b]WCT[/b], and supporting incident artifacts around the failure
[*]Starts every report, the viewer, and the share text with a [b]quick answer[/b]: what happened, the suspected mod and how likely it is, and one thing to try. The technical details stay below it, so you do not need WinDbg for the first pass
[*]Automatically reads CrashLogger logs when CrashLoggerSSE is installed
[*]Records the MO2 profile and mod list active at the crash, so the mods a report names are the ones you actually ran with
[*]Reads the game objects a crash was handling straight from the dump: type, FormID, name, the plugins that define and change it, and a reference's base object
[*]Explains freezes by what the game's main thread was waiting for: the GPU, the graphics driver, an error dialog a plugin opened, a game thread stuck in a plugin's code, or game threads waiting on each other's engine locks
[*]Says in plain words how an access violation happened: an object that was already gone (null pointer), a bad index, a jump into freed memory such as a removed hook, and so on
[*]Tells an error while you force-close the game (Alt+F4, the window's X button, Quit) apart from a CTD: the viewer does not pop up and it is not counted as a crash
[*]Records RAM, commit and VRAM at capture time, and warns about modules that hide crashes (such as SkyrimCrashGuard)
[*]Recognizes known causes and known engine crash spots that a public fix guards, and puts that fix first in the next steps
[/list]

[b][size=4]What It Does NOT Do[/size][/b]
[list]
[*]This is [b]not[/b] a crash prevention mod
[*]It does [b]not[/b] swallow exceptions or try to keep the game running after a fault
[*]It is a [b]diagnostics tool[/b] that helps you find likely causes and supporting clues
[*]There is no telemetry or auto-upload. Outputs stay [b]local only[/b]
[/list]

[b][size=4]Recommended Companion Mod[/size][/b]
[url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/59818]Crash Logger SSE AE VR — PDB support[/url]

If installed, Tullius auto-detects CrashLogger logs and includes them in the report.
It is not required, but it is strongly recommended for [b]richer diagnostics[/b].

[b][size=4]Key Included Files[/size][/b]
[code]
SKSE/Plugins/SkyrimDiag.dll            — SKSE plugin (in-game event recorder)
SKSE/Plugins/SkyrimDiag.ini            — Plugin settings
SKSE/Plugins/SkyrimDiagHelper.exe      — Out-of-process monitor (CTD / freeze / ILS detection)
SKSE/Plugins/SkyrimDiagHelper.ini      — Helper settings (thresholds, output, cleanup)
SKSE/Plugins/SkyrimDiagDumpToolCli.exe — Headless analyzer
SKSE/Plugins/SkyrimDiagWinUI/
  SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe          — Native viewer launcher
  app/
    SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe        — Self-contained viewer app
    SkyrimDiagDumpToolNative.dll       — Native analyzer engine
    ...                                — Bundled .NET / Windows App SDK runtime files
[/code]

[b][size=4]Requirements[/size][/b]
[list]
[*]Windows 10 build 19041 or later
[*]Skyrim Special Edition / Anniversary Edition: 1.5.97, 1.6.x or 1.7.104 (VR is not supported)
[*][url=https://skse.silverlock.org/]SKSE64[/url] for your game version (1.7.104 needs SKSE 2.3.1)
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/32444]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/url] (1.7.104 needs the v13 All in One)
[*][url=https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/windows/latest-supported-vc-redist]Visual C++ Redistributable 2015-2022 (x64)[/url]
[/list]

[i]WinUI runtime note:[/i] The [b]v0.2.52+[/b] release zip includes self-contained .NET 8 and Windows App SDK runtime files. You do not need to install .NET Desktop Runtime 8 or Windows App Runtime 1.8 separately.

If Windows asks to install Windows App Runtime 1.8 on v0.2.52+, an older or incomplete viewer is most likely being launched. Remove the existing [font=Courier New]SKSE\Plugins\SkyrimDiagWinUI[/font] folder completely, reinstall the latest zip, and check which mod provides these files in MO2.

[b]Important v0.2.79 Update Notice[/b]
Replace the previous installation with the complete v0.2.79 zip. Do not copy only individual EXEs or DLLs. The SKSE plugin, the Helper, the analyzer and the viewer all changed (the SharedLayout protocol is still v4), and there are no new settings. The plugin now reads SkyrimDiag.ini from beside SkyrimDiag.dll, and SkyrimDiag.log says whether it found it ([font=Courier New]loaded config from ...[/font] or [font=Courier New]config not found at ...; using defaults[/font]). Dumps analyzed by v0.2.78 or older are re-analyzed once when you open them in the viewer. If you tried v0.2.79-rc1, rc2 or rc3, the dumps they analyzed are not re-analyzed on their own (same version number): the rc2 and rc3 analysis is the same as this release; for a dump analyzed by rc1, select it and click [b]ANALYZE NOW[/b]. [b]If you update from v0.2.77:[/b] v0.2.78 changed the Helper, the analyzer and the viewer (a slow shutdown after a quit is not a freeze). [b]If you update from v0.2.76:[/b] v0.2.77 changed the Helper and the analyzer; form names for a FormID used as a pointer appear only in crash dumps captured by a v0.2.77 or newer Helper. [b]If you update from v0.2.75 or older:[/b] v0.2.76 added [font=Courier New]UnhandledCrashHoldMs[/font] in SkyrimDiag.ini (default 5000), and [font=Courier New]MaxForcedCloseCrashDumps[/font] (default 3) and [font=Courier New]KeepSlowShutdownHangDumps[/font] (default 0) in SkyrimDiagHelper.ini. Since v0.2.76 a crash that called an empty (null) function pointer gets a callstack and with it a new bucket key, so it does not continue the repeat-crash count of the same crash recorded before. [b]If you update from v0.2.74 or older:[/b] the Helper writes [font=Courier New]loaded_saves.json[/font] (the saves that finished loading) beside crash_history.json since v0.2.75. [b]If you update from v0.2.73 or older:[/b] the save being loaded is recorded only from the v0.2.74 plugin on, and since v0.2.74 [font=Courier New]CrashHookMode[/font] is read strictly (a typo or an out-of-range value means 1, the default, never 0 or 2). [b]If you update from v0.2.71 or older:[/b] on the first launch the dump folder is capped at 5 GB ([font=Courier New]MaxTotalDumpGB[/font], since v0.2.72) and the oldest dumps are deleted with their reports; move dumps you want to keep elsewhere first, or set [font=Courier New]MaxTotalDumpGB=0[/font] (unlimited) in the new SkyrimDiagHelper.ini. Since v0.2.68 the manual capture hotkey is [font=Courier New]Ctrl+Shift+Backspace[/font]. In MO2, install it as a new mod and disable the old one, or fully replace the old mod's files; do not merge a partial old viewer/runtime folder.
On Skyrim 1.5.97, v0.2.62 and v0.2.63 crash right after launch (SkyrimDiag.dll); fixed since v0.2.64.
On Skyrim 1.7.104, update SKSE to 2.3.1 and Address Library to the v13 All in One first. v0.2.61 and older do not load on 1.7.104 (SKSE shows a SkyrimDiag.dll popup); v0.2.62 and later fix that.

[b][size=4]Installation (MO2)[/size][/b]
[list=1]
[*]Install SKSE64, Address Library, and the Visual C++ Redistributable
[*]Install the zip as a mod in MO2 and enable it
[*]Launch Skyrim through SKSE
[*]The Helper attaches automatically. No separate manual launch is required
[/list]

[b]MO2: nothing appears after a crash?[/b]
MO2 runs Skyrim inside its USVFS virtual file system, and the Helper is started from the game process. MO2 users have reported that a mod-only install can silently miss the post-crash steps (no new dump/report, or the viewer never opens). First check [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiagHelper.log[/font] in the output folder. If it stops at the crash, users report this workaround:
[list=1]
[*]Copy the whole [font=Courier New]SKSE\Plugins\[/font] contents of the zip (including [font=Courier New]data\[/font] and the complete [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiagWinUI\[/font] folder) into the real game [font=Courier New]Data\SKSE\Plugins\[/font] folder
[*]Disable the Tullius mod in MO2 so only one copy is used
[*]Set [font=Courier New]OutputDir=[/font] in the real [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiagHelper.ini[/font] to an absolute folder outside the game and MO2 folders (e.g. [font=Courier New]C:\SkyrimDiag[/font])
[*]When updating, replace the whole physical copy and do not mix releases
[/list]

[b]Linux (Wine / Proton)[/b]
Capture and the text report work under Wine/Proton, but the WinUI viewer does not: it needs the Windows App Runtime, which Wine does not provide. Read [font=Courier New]*_SkyrimDiagReport.txt[/font] in the output folder instead. Starting with v0.2.59 the Helper detects Wine and writes a report for every capture; on earlier versions set [font=Courier New]AutoOpenViewerOnCrash=0[/font] and [font=Courier New]AutoOpenViewerOnHang=0[/font] in [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiagHelper.ini[/font].

[b][size=4]How To Use[/size][/b]

[b]Automatic capture[/b]
[list]
[*][b]CTD:[/b] For captured fatal crashes, Tullius normally writes a crash dump and report. Strong faults followed by process exit code 0 are filtered by default and leave clean-exit evidence metadata instead, unless the plugin saw the exception go unhandled (it reached the process's top-level exception filter): that one is kept as a crash, or only its dump when the game ran on for over a minute after it
[*][b]Freeze / ILS:[/b] When the configured threshold is exceeded, Tullius generates a hang dump
[/list]

[b]Manual snapshot[/b]
You can capture a manual snapshot with [font=Courier New]Ctrl+Shift+Backspace[/font] (change it with [font=Courier New]ManualCaptureHotkey=[/font] in SkyrimDiagHelper.ini). Up to v0.2.68-rc3 it was [font=Courier New]Ctrl+Shift+F12[/font], which Crash Logger 1.25 also uses for its thread dump; that thread dump can freeze the game, so do not set the hotkey back to a chord that holds Crash Logger's keys.

[i]Note:[/i] A snapshot taken during normal gameplay may show [b]low confidence[/b].
It is most useful when the game is already stuck or behaving abnormally.

[b]Viewing results (WinUI Viewer)[/b]
[list]
[*]Depending on your settings, the viewer may auto-open after a CTD
[*]To open manually, run [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe[/font], select a dump, and click [b]Analyze[/b]
[*]You can also launch it from the command line:
[/list]
[code]
SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe "path\to\dump.dmp"
[/code]

The viewer typically shows:
[list]
[*]Summary
[*]Actionable candidates
[*]Evidence list
[*]Event timeline
[*]Resource log
[*]Wait Chain (WCT)
[/list]

[b][size=4]What's New in v0.2.79[/size][/b]
Mod DLLs in the game folder that carry a Windows DLL name are judged by where they loaded from, the viewer's summary got buttons and selectable text, the plugin reads its INI beside its DLL, and old dumps are deleted on long output paths.[list]
[*][b]Mod DLLs with Windows names:[/b] a mod DLL in the game folder named like a Windows DLL, such as OptiScaler's winmm.dll, loads next to System32's copy of the same name. The analyzer filtered it by name as a system DLL, so even when Crash Logger said the crash was inside it, the report said the signals were split with another mod. It now checks where the DLL was loaded from and treats it as a mod. Re-analyzing the 46 kept dumps changed only the two winmm.dll crashes: one now names winmm.dll as the crash location (medium) and asks to compare the nearby DLLs Crash Logger listed; the other, a crash reported in a system DLL, now lists winmm.dll as a medium candidate from Crash Logger's frame instead of a high suspect
[*][b]DirectX-named DLLs in the game folder:[/b] d3d11.dll, dxgi.dll, d3d9.dll and d3d12.dll from ENB, ReShade or DXVK were already judged by path as candidates; now the freeze's "waiting on the GPU" check and the quick answer treat them as mods too. A freeze inside such a DLL with no graphics driver frames can name that DLL instead of saying the game waited on the GPU (not yet seen in a real dump)
[*][b]Viewer:[/b] the summary text can be selected and copied. [b]Open Crash Logger log[/b] (shown when the paired Crash Logger log is found; it opens .log files only) and [b]Open report folder[/b] sit above the summary, and the raw data tab has [b]Copy WCT[/b] and [b]Copy report[/b]. The status moved to a bar at the bottom of the window, so it shows on every tab. The recent dump list shows up to 30 dumps (was 12), and picking one not analyzed yet starts at once instead of waiting up to 1.5 s
[*][b]Plugin settings:[/b] SkyrimDiag.ini is read from beside SkyrimDiag.dll, also when it is saved as UTF-8 (with a BOM) or sits under a non-English or long MO2 path. SkyrimDiag.log says whether it was loaded or the defaults are used
[*][b]Long output paths:[/b] with an output folder path of about 200 characters or more (a deeply placed MO2 or game folder), old dumps over the retention limits ([font=Courier New]MaxCrashDumps[/font], [font=Courier New]MaxHangDumps[/font], ...) were never deleted. Dumps and their side files are now deleted whatever the path length
[*][b]Preflight:[/b] SkyrimDiag_Preflight.json gains [font=Courier New]BINARY_VERSION_CONSISTENCY[/font] (the plugin, the analyzer and the viewer's analysis DLL have the Helper's version) and [font=Courier New]ADDRESS_LIBRARY_PRESENT[/font] (the Address Library for the running game version is there), to catch a mixed install
[*][b]Fixes:[/b] a crash two Helpers in a row could not finish is no longer recorded when the game then exits normally (exit code 0); a quit flag set together with Alt+F4 now counts as a quit (no 10 s limit for a later crash); the exception line says write for a write-only instruction at a non-canonical address (#GP) and decodes rep movs/stos and AVX/AVX2 memory instructions; a wait-chain cycle makes even a manual capture a freeze; reading crash object memory no longer touches the game's guard pages
[/list]

[b][size=4]Earlier: v0.2.78[/size][/b]
A slow shutdown after qqq or a menu Quit is no longer reported as a freeze, and a crash after the game was asked to quit is a forced close however late it comes.[list]
[*][b]Slow shutdown:[/b] a report on a Korean community said every qqq quit left a freeze capture ("stuck on a loading screen, suspected mod usvfs_x64.dll, medium"); the game took over two minutes to close. Once the Helper sees the game's quit flag (console qqq, a menu Quit, or a forced quit), a later freeze is held as a possible slow shutdown, whatever menu or loading screen is up. If the game then exits normally (exit code 0), the dump is deleted and no viewer opens ([font=Courier New]KeepSlowShutdownHangDumps=1[/font] keeps it). Any other exit, such as ending the game from Task Manager, keeps and analyzes the dump as before
[*][b]Slow shutdown in the report:[/b] such a dump analyzed by v0.2.78 is classified as a slow shutdown. The quick answer says the game took long to close after you chose to quit and that it is not a freeze during play; it names no suspect mod, and the dump is not added to the crash history. The viewer shows "Slow shutdown — not a freeze in play". The quit flag record must still be in the blackbox
[*][b]Crash after quitting:[/b] a crash any time after the quit flag is a forced close (not a CTD, not in the crash history, no viewer pop-up). It used to count only within 10 s of the close request, so a crash late in a slow shutdown read as a CTD. Alt+F4, the window's X button and close messages from other programs keep the 10 s window, because the game may have ignored them
[*]The viewer re-analyzes dumps analyzed by v0.2.77 once when you open them
[/list]

[b][size=4]Earlier: v0.2.77[/size][/b]
The same answer for a crash inside a mod DLL with or without Crash Logger, the form behind a FormID used as a pointer, and no wrong mod for a crash while loading a Papyrus script.[list]
[*][b]Crash Logger no longer weakens the answer:[/b] a crash inside a mod DLL whose callstack also points at that DLL was rated medium without a Crash Logger log and low with one, so users of the recommended companion mod got the weaker answer. Both are medium now: Crash Logger's frame repeats where the exception happened, so it neither raises nor lowers the likelihood. The conclusion, quick answer, evidence and recommendations all say the same thing
[*][b]FormID used as a pointer:[/b] when a mod puts a form's FormID where a pointer to the form belongs, the crash register holds a small value such as 0x12EB6. The Helper looks the crash registers up in the game's form table and keeps the form in the dump, and the report names it, e.g. [font=Courier New]rax=0x12EB6 -> bad_pointer (rax holds the FormID of TESObjectARMO "Iron Shield" (FormID 0x00012EB6, Skyrim.esm))[/font]. The quick answer says it seems to have crashed using a form's FormID as if it were a pointer. Two crashes on November 15 and 16 (OSLAroused.dll, register 0x12EB6) led to this
[*][b]Papyrus script loading crashes:[/b] when the crashing thread's callstack is game and Windows code all the way to the thread start, the analyzer no longer scans the stack memory for leftover mod addresses. On November 17 that scan named Animation Queue Fix for a crash caused by a script (.pex) with a docstring over 4096 bytes. A crash near the game's Papyrus script loader now gets its own quick answer: it seems to have been reading a Papyrus script; try the mods you added or updated recently and any script compiled anew, one at a time
[*][b]Text over a pointer:[/b] when a register that should hold an address holds printable text (8 ASCII or 4 UTF-16 characters), the report shows it, e.g. [font=Courier New](rax holds the text "lls are ")[/font]; searching the mod folders for that text found the broken script at once
[*][b]Weak clues say so:[/b] a candidate that only the stack scan found is described as only seen nearby in memory when the game crashed
[*]The viewer re-analyzes dumps analyzed by v0.2.76 once when you open them
[/list]

[b][size=4]Earlier: v0.2.76[/size][/b]
No stall on every repeat of a handled exception, an unhandled crash reported at once, game function IDs, and a callstack for a call through an empty function pointer.[list]
[*][b]Repeated handled exceptions:[/b] when the same exception (code and address) has been handled by the game twice in a session, the Helper first checks whether the game keeps running and writes nothing if it does. Each of those dumps froze every game thread for about a second (a Nexus report measured a 1,162 ms stop on v0.2.73; the reporter's Helper log had the same access violation 14 times in 23 minutes). The first two still write a dump
[*][b]Unhandled crashes at once:[/b] when an exception that nothing handled reaches the process's top-level exception filter, the plugin tells the Helper and holds the crashing thread only until the dump is written ([font=Courier New]UnhandledCrashHoldMs[/font], default 5000 ms; in game the hold took 1.4 to 1.7 s); then Crash Logger writes its log as usual. A real crash at an address that had been handled twice is dumped too, a crash is confirmed without the 3 s heartbeat wait, and a crash on a thread that does not block the game loop is no longer released as handled
[*][b]Crashes at exit code 0:[/b] a crash the plugin saw go unhandled is kept even when the game ends with exit code 0 (a crash on another thread while closing the game), or only its dump when the game ran on for over a minute
[*][b]Without Crash Logger:[/b] the plugin checks once a second that its top-level exception filter is still in place and takes it back, so unhandled C++ exceptions and breakpoints are no longer lost when something replaced it
[*][b]Empty function pointer calls:[/b] the callstack is walked from the return address the call left on the stack, after checking that a call instruction ends right before it, so it starts at the caller instead of being empty. The November 15 Light Placer crash now shows the same stack as Crash Logger and names Light Placer with high likelihood. Such crashes get a new bucket key
[*][b]Game function IDs:[/b] game frames show their Address Library ID, e.g. [font=Courier New]SkyrimSE.exe+0x49fb54 (ID 29895+0xe4)[/font], only when the function start matches an ID exactly
[*][b]Callstack from the top:[/b] the report's callstack starts at the faulting frame instead of two frames above the first plugin frame; a stack that ends in hook code says so and where the hook's recorded jump goes
[*][b]Driver errors just before a crash:[/b] the Helper writes the graphics driver record beside a crash dump, and a driver error within 60 s before the crash comes first in the quick answer, with the suspected mod second
[*][b]Forced-close dumps counted apart:[/b] dumps of an error while the game was being closed have their own limit ([font=Courier New]MaxForcedCloseCrashDumps[/font], default 3) and no longer push out real crash dumps
[*][b]Closing is not a freeze:[/b] a game already closing with one thread left gets no freeze dump, and a freeze caught in a menu that never recovered before a clean exit is treated as a slow shutdown and deleted ([font=Courier New]KeepSlowShutdownHangDumps=1[/font] keeps it)
[*][b]Manual capture of a stuck game:[/b] analyzed as a freeze when the heartbeat had stopped for over 5 s and the game window was in front or not responding
[*][b].NET runtime:[/b] [font=Courier New]clr.dll[/font] and the other .NET runtime files are Windows components, not suspected mods
[*][b]Helper watchdog:[/b] when the Helper keeps exiting right after start, the plugin waits 1, 2, 4 and 8 s and gives up after five failures in a row
[*]The viewer re-analyzes dumps analyzed by v0.2.75 once when you open them
[/list]

[b][size=4]Older Versions[/size][/b]
Changes in v0.2.75 and earlier are in the [url=https://github.com/servaltullius/Tullius_ctd_loger/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md]changelog[/url] and the [url=https://github.com/servaltullius/Tullius_ctd_loger/releases]GitHub releases[/url].

[i]Real use:[/i] each release is played on one MO2 setup (game 1.6.1170, SKSE 2.2.8, Crash Logger 1.27) before it ships.
For v0.2.77, the release candidates ran on the same MO2 setup with Crash Logger 1.27 (November 16 and 17). A real OSLAroused.dll crash while loading a save was rated medium by rc1, with the conclusion that Crash Logger's frame and the stack both point at that DLL. With the rc2 package, which has the same code as this release, the Helper logged the form table it uses, and a development-only test plugin that reads a form through its FormID crashed with the report naming the form (as above) and its plugin, which matched the record's last override. The Papyrus script loader crashes were checked by re-analyzing the two real November 17 dumps; they stopped after the script was fixed. Not yet seen in real use: the text-over-pointer line and the script loader answer on a dump captured by the new Helper, and 1.5.97 and 1.7.x.
For v0.2.78, the release candidate package, which has the same code as this release, ran on the same MO2 setup with Crash Logger and Engine Fixes' safe exit (November 18). The plugin logged v0.2.78.0. Two sessions closed with a menu Quit left the Helper's "The game was asked to quit" line and exited normally without a capture, and a session closed with Alt+F4 was logged as a close request without that line, as intended. None of those sessions wrote a dump. With the safe exit the game closes within a second, so a slow shutdown could not be reproduced here. Not yet seen in real use: a slow shutdown held and then deleted, a crash more than 10 s after the quit flag (both covered by tests), and 1.5.97 and 1.7.x.
For v0.2.79, the rc3 package, which has the same code as this release, ran seven times on the same MO2 setup (November 22). Every session exited normally with no preflight warnings, the plugin logged that it loaded SkyrimDiag.ini and v0.2.79.0, and a start that took 1 min 49 s to load its data left no freeze capture. The rc3 viewer opened a November 19 crash dump, and Open Crash Logger log opened its paired log. Not yet seen in real use: the exit after a Helper gave up on a crash, retention on a long output path, a new crash in a game-folder winmm.dll or d3d11.dll (checked by re-analysis only), the new instruction decoding, the quit flag record with Alt+F4, and 1.5.97 and 1.7.x.

[b][size=4]Where To Find The Viewer (MO2)[/size][/b]
In MO2, right-click the mod and choose [b]Open in Explorer[/b].

Then go to:
[code]
SKSE\Plugins\SkyrimDiagWinUI\SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe
[/code]

[i]Tip:[/i] A desktop shortcut is convenient if you open reports often.

[b][size=4]Capture Types[/size][/b]
[code]
Situation              Dump pattern           Trigger
─────────────────────  ─────────────────────  ──────────────────────────
CTD                    *_Crash_*.dmp          Automatic
Freeze / ILS           *_Hang_*.dmp           Automatic (threshold)
Manual snapshot        *_Manual_*.dmp         Ctrl+Shift+Backspace
[/code]

[b][size=4]Output Location[/size][/b]
[list]
[*][b]Default:[/b] Leave [font=Courier New]OutputDir=[/font] blank to use MO2 [font=Courier New]overwrite\SKSE\Plugins\Tullius Ctd Logs\[/font]
[*][b]Custom:[/b] Set [font=Courier New]OutputDir=[/font] in [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiagHelper.ini[/font] using a plain path without quotes. Absolute paths work; relative paths are resolved from the helper folder.
[/list]

[i]Important:[/i] Recent versions use the [b]Tullius output location[/b] as the primary discovery path instead of generic Windows [font=Courier New]CrashDumps[/font].

[b][size=4]Important Settings (SkyrimDiagHelper.ini)[/size][/b]
[code]
; Freeze / ILS thresholds (seconds)
HangThresholdInGameSec=10
HangThresholdInMenuSec=30
HangThresholdLoadingSec=600
EnableAdaptiveLoadingThreshold=1

; Viewer auto-open
AutoOpenViewerOnCrash=1
AutoOpenCrashOnlyIfProcessExited=1

; Dumps: the game's code and the code the stacks point at
; (DumpAllModuleCode=1: every module's code, 0.5-0.9 GB a dump)
DumpMode=1
DumpAllModuleCode=0

; Cleanup (0 = unlimited)
MaxCrashDumps=20
; Dumps of an error while the game was being closed, counted apart
MaxForcedCloseCrashDumps=3
MaxHangDumps=20
MaxManualDumps=20
MaxEtwTraces=5
; Total size of all dumps, in GB
MaxTotalDumpGB=5
[/code]

[b][size=4]Performance[/size][/b]
This is designed as [b]event recording + on-demand dump capture[/b], not heavy continuous monitoring.

[list]
[*][font=Courier New]EnableResourceLog=1[/font] — records loose-file opens (.nif/.hkx/.tri). If you suspect overhead, test with [font=Courier New]=0[/font] first
[*][font=Courier New]EnablePerfHitchLog=1[/font] — logs main-thread stalls. This is relatively lightweight
[*][font=Courier New]CrashHookMode=1[/font] — keep this at 1. Mode 2 is not recommended
[/list]

[b][size=4]Language[/size][/b]
The UI follows your Windows language by default.
You can override it with launch options:
[code]
SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe --lang en
SkyrimDiagDumpToolWinUI.exe --lang ko
[/code]

[b][size=4]FAQ[/size][/b]

[b]Q: The viewer popped up during save/load, but the game kept running.[/b]
A: An exception during save/cosave may be internally handled, so the game can continue.
In that case, the shown suspect is [b]a warning signal[/b], not a confirmed root cause.
To reduce popups, use [font=Courier New]AutoOpenCrashOnlyIfProcessExited=1[/font] or [font=Courier New]AutoOpenViewerOnCrash=0[/font].

[b]Q: I closed the game with Alt+F4, an error was recorded, but the viewer did not open.[/b]
A: That is intended from v0.2.70. An error within 10 seconds of Alt+F4, the window's X button or Quit is an error during a forced close, not a CTD while playing; saves are usually not affected. The dump and report are still in the output folder. Set [font=Courier New]EnableCloseRequestLog=0[/font] in [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiag.ini[/font] to report it as a CTD again.

[b]Q: Where are the dump files?[/b]
A: With [font=Courier New]OutputDir=[/font] left blank, new dumps go to MO2 [font=Courier New]overwrite\SKSE\Plugins\Tullius Ctd Logs\[/font].
You can collect them in another folder by setting [font=Courier New]OutputDir=[/font].
Write the path as plain text without quotes. Absolute paths work; relative paths are resolved from the helper folder.

[b]Q: Do I need CrashLoggerSSE?[/b]
A: No, but it is recommended. If present, Tullius can produce richer reports.

[b]Q: Does freeze analysis work automatically?[/b]
A: Yes. The report says what the game's main thread was waiting for (the GPU, the driver, an error dialog, the engine or a game thread stuck in a plugin) and uses WCT, the blackbox and the loading context alongside it.

[b]Q: I get a SkyrimDiag.dll popup on Skyrim 1.7.104.[/b]
A: Install v0.2.62 or later, with SKSE 2.3.1 and the Address Library v13 All in One. Older versions of this mod do not load on 1.7.104.

[b]Q: My 1.6.1170 game closes right after launch since I also ran 1.7.104.[/b]
A: Both versions share [font=Courier New]%LOCALAPPDATA%\Skyrim Special Edition\ContentCatalog.txt[/font], and 1.7.104 rewrites it in a format 1.6.1170 cannot read. Move that file to the Recycle Bin; the game makes a new one. From v0.2.63 the report says this when the crash matches.

[b]Q: On 1.5.97 the game crashes right after launch, and the report points at SkyrimDiag.dll.[/b]
A: That is a bug in v0.2.62 and v0.2.63 on 1.5.97. Install v0.2.64 (the whole zip). On an older version you can also set [font=Courier New]EnableResourceLog=0[/font] in [font=Courier New]SkyrimDiag.ini[/font] to skip the hook that crashes.

[b][size=4]Privacy[/size][/b]
[list]
[*]Dumps and logs may contain [b]local file paths[/b] and [b]usernames[/b]
[*]crash_history.json and loaded_saves.json in the output folder hold save names
[*]Review and redact before sharing publicly
[*]Analysis results are best-effort estimates, not a guaranteed verdict
[/list]

[b][size=4]Support / Bug Reports[/size][/b]
When reporting an issue, include as many of these as possible:
[list]
[*][font=Courier New].dmp[/font] file
[*][font=Courier New]*_SkyrimDiagReport.txt[/font]
[*][font=Courier New]*_SkyrimDiagSummary.json[/font]
[*][font=Courier New]SkyrimDiag_Incident_*.json[/font] if present
[*][font=Courier New]*_SkyrimDiagBlackbox.jsonl[/font] if present
[*]CrashLogger logs if present
[/list]

